<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tages was a figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscan-religion">Etruscan religion</a>, a child with the wisdom of an old man who, according to legend, sprang from a ploughed furrow near Tarquinii. He was said to have revealed the Etruscan discipline of divination, including the reading of entrails by haruspices, to the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>. Roman writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> recorded the tale, and books of ritual attributed to him formed part of the Etrusca disciplina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tages.jpg" alt="Tages" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarquinia_-_Ara_della_Regina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tages is known only from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> authors of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the Empire, whose accounts of him are quasimythical. Versions of his birth vary: he is sometimes the grandson of Jove, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>'s telling a Tyrrhenian ploughman saw a clod turn into a man who spoke of things destined to happen and of how the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> could learn the future. Since the Etruscan alphabet had no G, the name is most likely a Latin form of an Etruscan word.</p><p>A bronze mirror of the third century BCE from Tuscania shows a young haruspex in a conical hat examining a liver, labelled pavatarchies, while an older, bearded haruspex labelled avl tarchunus listens. Massimo Pallottino read the first label as pava Tarchies, the child Tarchies, and the second as the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarchon">Tarchon</a>, the king of Tarquinia in legend and a founder of the Etruscan League; Giulio Mauro Facchetti instead linked the name to an Etruscan stem thac-, which he takes to mean voice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Tages was held to have revealed a cosmic view of divinity and the right ways of learning the divine will on matters of public concern. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called the texts recording his revelations, and those of a few other prophets, chiefly the female figure Vegoia, the Etrusca Disciplina. They were written in Etruscan despite their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> titles, and none survives. The last writer who claimed to have read them, John the Lydian, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the sixth century CE, described a dialogue in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarchon">Tarchon</a> questions Tages, the answers written in ancient letters that he found hard to understand; he suggested that Tages is only a parable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Labelled Etruscan images of Tages are very rare. Figures with the augur's crooked staff, the lituus, or with the haruspex's conical cap are common but need not be Tages, and art historians' identifications are speculative. Engraved gemstones of the fourth century BCE, once set in seal rings, seem to show the myth: a bearded figure, perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarchon">Tarchon</a>, bends to listen to a beardless figure rising from the ground. A bronze votive statuette of the third century BCE from Tarquinia shows a sitting infant with an adult's head and face.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revelations of the prophet <b>Tages</b>, the Libri Tagetici, included the rules of divination from animal entrails and discussion of the afterlife and its rituals, the Libri Acherontici.</blockquote><a href="/religion/etruscan-religion">Etruscan religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Their religion rested on sacred books, the Etrusca disciplina, revealed according to tradition by <b>Tages</b>, a child prophet who sprang from a ploughed furrow near Tarquinia, and by the nymph Vegoia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In legend it was founded by Tarchon, and the haruspex <b>Tages</b>, who revealed Etruscan religious lore, appeared in its fields.</blockquote><a href="/state/tarquinii">Tarquinii</a></li></ul></section>
